PHP serialization, PHP object injection, and misplaced trust

By letting the client store the serialized PHP object and trusting the
client not to modify the object, the server is vulnerable to rogue
object injection.  We can inject a "Logger" object into the drawing
which will be unpacked by the PHP server and used directly.  With our
injected Logger object, the __destroy() function outputs an
exit message to a log file upon completion of the script.  Both of these
are stored in the object that we have control over.  So, set the log file
to point to an accessible directory on the server (img/myphp.php) and
then set the exit message to "passthru("cat /etc/natas_webpass/natas27").
Then, access the PHP script directly to get the desired password.

Note that the directory contains PHP code to output the Logger object
we want.  The python code uses the output of the PHP script to set the
drawing field.

